1 NAME Nikolaus /von Salm-Neuburg/
2 GIVN Nikolaus
2 _MARNM von Salm-Neuburg
2 NICK Miklós III.
1 NAME Nikolaus II, Graf von Salm und Neuburg
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1503
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 MAR 1550
2 ADDR
3 CITY Eger
3 STAE Heves County
3 CTRY Hungary
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00042459&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY Nikolaus was born in 1503, the son of Nikolaus I, Graf von Salm in Neuburg am Inn, and Freiin Elisabeth von Rogendorff. In 1524 he married Gräfin Aemiliane von Eberstein, daughter of Bernhard III, Graf von Eberstein in N
2 CONC eueberstein, and Gräfin Kunigunde von Waldburg-Sonnenberg. Their four children Lukretia, Nikolaus III, Egino and Julius reached their maturity. After the death of Aemiliane, in 1540 Nikolaus married Margarethe Szecsy von Felso-Lin
2 CONC dva, daughter of Thomas Szecsy von Felso-Lindva and Magdalena Szekely von Ormosd. Their daughter Maria Magdalena would have progeny, marrying Ladislav III 'starsi' z Lobkowicz z Zbirov.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikolaus commissioned the Austrian painter and printmaker Wolf Huber (1485-1553) to redesign Neuburg Palace on the Inn River, not far from the town, only fragments of which survive. These indicate the addition of wings in the styl
2 CONC e of the Italian Renaissance, complete with rich decorative paintings. Huber probably also designed the reliefs on the tomb of Nikolaus II's father Nikolaus I, now located in the Votivkirche in Vienna. For his work on the palace H
2 CONC uber was awarded a pension and a manor house, Neufils.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his father, Nikolaus had a distinguished career in imperial service, which led to his induction into the Order of the Golden Fleece in 1531. In 1526, on the death of Lajos II, king of Hungary and Bohemia, he had been succeede
2 CONC d by Ferdinand I, brother of Emperor Charles V. After the death in 1540 of Jan Zapolya, the pretender to the throne of Hungary. Ferdinand I, now the emperor-elect, sent Nikolaus on a mission to Zapolya's widow, Princess Elzbieta o
2 CONC f Poland, the daughter of King Zygmunt I of Poland, to reassert Ferdinand's rights as king of Hungary under the terms of the Treaty of Grosswardein of 1538. Under the influence of the Hungarian chancellor, Cardinal György Martinuz
2 CONC zi, and Elzbieta's father, her under-age son Jan Zygmunt Zapolya had been crowned anti-king, under the regency of his mother and Martinuzzi.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1546 Nikolaus was appointed imperial commander-in-chief in Hungary and Croatia-Slavonia. He controlled the defence of the country and started to fashion a new border defence system against the Turks, which was completed after h
2 CONC is death at Erlau on 5 March 1550.
