1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ==Links:==
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://geneall.net/de/name/48889/philipp-ludwig-ii-graf-von-hanau-munzenberg/ geneall]
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Louis_II,_Count_of_Hanau-M%C3%BCnzenberg Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip Louis II of Hanau-Münzenberg (18 November 1576 Hanau - 9 August 1612 Hanau), was one of the most notable counts of Hanau of the early modern period, his policies bringing about sweeping changes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Philip Louis II of Hanau-Münzenberg was born in the castle at Hanau and baptised two weeks later on 3 December. His parents were Count Philip Louis I of Hanau-Münzenberg (1553-1580) and Magdalena, Countess of Waldeck (1558-1
2 CONC 599).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Guardianship
2 CONT
2 CONT He nominally succeeded his deceased father on 4 February 1580, albeit under the guardianship of John VI, Count of Nassau-Dillenburg (1536-1606), Count Ludwig I of Sayn-Wittgenstein (1568-1607) and Count Philipp IV of Hanau -Lichte
2 CONC nberg (1514-1590), replaced in 1585 by his son, Count Philipp V von Hanau-Lichtenberg (1541-1599). The guardianship ended only in 1608.
2 CONT
2 CONT His widowed mother, Magdalena, was remarried on 9 December 1581 to Count Johann VII, the Middle, of Nassau-Siegen (1561-1623), the son of one of the guardians. In consequence Philip Louis II and his younger brother, Count Albrecht
2 CONC , joined the Nassau-Dillenburg court, a centre of the Reformation movement in Germany and closely tied to the Electoral Palatinate of the Rhine. The new ideas he encountered here greatly influenced his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT The guardianship was ended only in 1608 at the behest of the Elector Friedrich IV (1574–1610).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT During a wedding feast in Dillenburg that lasted from 23 October 1596 - 3 November 1596, he married Catherine Belgica, producing the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlotte Louise (1597–1649 Kassel), not married
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter (29 July 1598 - 9 August 1598), died unbaptised
2 CONT
2 CONT Philipp Ulrich (2 January 1601 - 7 April 1604 Steinau)
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalia Elisabeth (1602–1651 Kassel), married to Wilhelm V of Hesse-Kassel
2 CONT
2 CONT Katharina Juliane (1604–1668 Hanau), married first on 11 September 1631 to Count Albert Otto II. of Solms-Laubach, Rödelheim and Assenheim and second on 31 March 1642 to Moritz Christian von Wied-Runkel
2 CONT
2 CONT Philipp Moritz (1605–1638), buried in the Marienkirche in Hanau
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm Reinhard (1607–1630 Aachen), buried in the Marienkirche in Hanau
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich Ludwig (1609–1632) died during the Siege of Maastricht
2 CONT
2 CONT Friedrich Ludwig (27 July 1610 - 4 October 1628 Paris), buried in the family tomb of the Duke of Bouillon in Sedan
2 CONT
2 CONT Jakob Johann (1612–1636 Zabern), buried in St. Nicholas in Strasbourg
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT This article incorporates information from the German Wikipedia.
