2 NSFX Countess of Derby
2 _MARNM Stanley
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1599
2 PLAC Thouars, Deux Sevres, France
2 ADDR
1 BURI
2 DATE 6 APR 1644
2 PLAC Ormskirk, Lancashire, England, United Kingdom
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 21 MAR 1664
2 ADDR
3 CITY Knowsley
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 TITL Lady
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00004123&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlotte Stanley, Countess of Derby (1599–1664), born Charlotte de La Trémoille, was the daughter of the French nobleman Claude de La Trémoille, Duke of Thouars, and his wife Charlotte Brabantina of Nassau. Her maternal grandpare
2 CONC nts were William the Silent and Charlotte de Bourbon.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 26 June 1626, Charlotte married the English nobleman James Stanley, 7th Earl of Derby, who was taken prisoner at Worcester in 1651, and was beheaded at Bolton. Lady Derby was famous for her defence of Lathom House in the Siege 
2 CONC of Lathom House by Parliamentary forces during the First English Civil War in 1644. Her husband was also Lord of Mann, and her attempt to barter the Isle of Man for her husband's freedom provoked an anti-English revolt on the isla
2 CONC nd led by Illiam Dhone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlotte and Derby were parents of four daughters and five sons. Only four of their children appear to have survived to a marriageable age:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Charles Stanley, 8th Earl of Derby (19 January 1628 – 21 December 1672).
2 CONT *Lady Henriette Mary Stanley (17 November 1630 – 27 December 1685). Married William Wentworth, 2nd Earl of Strafford.
2 CONT *Lady Amelia Ann Sophia Stanley. Married John Murray, 1st Marquess of Atholl.
2 CONT *Lady Catherine Stanley. Married Henry Pierrepont, 1st Marquess of Dorchester.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles's two sons, William, the 9th Earl (c. 1655–1702), and James, the 10th Earl (1664–1736), both died without sons, and consequently, when James died in February 1736, his titles and estates passed to Sir Edward Stanley (1689–
2 CONC 1776), a descendant of the 1st earl. From him the later Earls of Derby are descended.
2 CONT
2 CONT External links
2 CONT *History of the Countess of Derby
2 CONT *This article incorporates text from the public domain 1907 edition of The Nuttall Encyclopædia.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlotte_Stanley,_Countess_of_Derby
2 CONT *See also http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlotte_de_La_Tr%C3%A9moille
2 CONT *__________________
2 CONT *''''''Charlotte de la Trémoille1
2 CONT *''''''F, #13886, d. 21 March 1663/64
2 CONT *Last Edited=20 Feb 2010
2 CONT *''''''    Charlotte de la Trémoille was the daughter of Claude de la Trémoille, Duc de Thouars and Charlotte von Nassau-Dillenburg.1,3 She married James Stanley, 7th Earl of Derby, son of William Stanley, 6th Earl of Derby and La
2 CONC dy Elizabeth de Vere, on 26 June 1626.3 She died on 21 March 1663/64.3
2 CONT *''''''      Her married name became Stanley. As a result of her marriage, Charlotte de la Trémoille was styled as Countess of Derby on 29 September 1642. In 1644 she defended Lathom House against Parliamentary troops.3 In 1646 sh
2 CONC e again defended Lathom House.3
2 CONT *''''''Children of Charlotte de la Trémoille and James Stanley, 7th Earl of Derby
2 CONT **1.Lady Amelia Anne Sophia Stanley+1 d. 22 Feb 1702/3
2 CONT **2.Lady Henrietta Maria Stanley4
2 CONT **3.Charles Stanley, 8th Earl of Derby+3 b. 19 Jan 1628, d. 21 Dec 1672
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extin
2 CONC ct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 316. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *2.[S3409] Caroline Maubois, "re: Penancoet Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 2 December 2008. Hereinafter cited as "re: Penancoet Family."
2 CONT *3.[S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 1102. Hereinafter cited as Burke's P
2 CONC eerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *4.[S22] Sir Bernard Burke, C.B. LL.D., A Genealogical History of the Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire, new edition (1883; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland: Genealogical Publishing Company, 1978)
2 CONC , page 577. Hereinafter cited as Burkes Extinct Peerage.
