1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Source: Ella Zerbey Elliott's Blue Book of Schuylkill County, 1916 (published prior to 1923, copyright has expired)
2 CONT
2 CONT The history of the Zerbes in the United States begins with that of the three immigrants, John Phillip, Mardin and Lorenz, who sailed from Rotterdam the day before Christmas, 1709, for the Province of New York, in the English colon
2 CONC ies, of North America.  They came on the same vessel with John Conrad Weiser.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were three men of the first generation of immigrants, 1710, Martin, John Phillip and Lorenz Zerbe. Of the male children of Martin and Lorenz, who settled in Berks County, as far as known, there were nine men of the second ge
2 CONC neration.
2 CONT
2 CONT =======
2 CONT
2 CONT ZERBE-ZERBY FAMILY
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 717
2 CONT
2 CONT Surnames: ZERBE, ZERBY, ZERVE, ZERWE, ZERBEN, KELLER, WAGNER, OAKS, FRYMOYER, MOLL, MOYER, GERBER, TYSON, SCHMEHL, WERNER, MENGEL, EPLER, SNYDER, De LONG
2 CONT
2 CONT Zerbe/Zerby (also Zerve, Zerwe and Zerben). The Zerbe family of Berks was very early settled in America. The original home of the family was in France, but owing to their steadfast loyalty to their religious faith they were oblige
2 CONC d to find homes elsewhere, that they might worship as they thought right. On their first coming to the New World they settled in New York State, in the Schoharie Valley and a little farther south at Livingston Manor, from which pl
2 CONC aces they followed the migratory tide into the fertile valley of the Tulpehocken. Rupp in his "30,000 Names of Immigrants", shows a Lorenz Zerbe who came from Schoharie to Tulpehocken in 1723, and in addition to Lorenz mentions a 
2 CONC John Philip Zerbe and a Martin Zerbe among those above twenty-one years of age, who passed the winter of 1710 and summer of 1711 in Livingston Manor, N.Y., and who may have come to Tulpehocken at a later period. The name of John o
2 CONC f Johan has been a favorite one in the family, as appears from the tax lists and vital statistics.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Zerbe, born in North Heidelberg township June 20, 1799, died in Reading in 1874, and is buried at Little Tulpehocken church. He was a tailor by trade, working at that occupation in different parts of the county, and for some 
2 CONC years also engaged in farming. In politics he was a Democrat. He was a member of St. Daniel's Church. His wife, whose maiden name was Martha Keller, died in Penn township in 1871, aged sixty-three years. She was a daughter of John
2 CONC  Keller. Their children were: Elias, born Aug. 4, 1822, died March 25, 1906; Rebecca m. John Wagner, and both are deceased; Catherine m. Jeremiah Oaks, and both are deceased; Urias is mentioned below; William K., born Nov. 12, 183
2 CONC 7, in North Heidelberg, enlisted Sept. 2, 1862, in Company G, 151st Pa. V.I., served ten months, and now resides in Reading, unmarried; Sarah m. Jonathan Frymoyer, deceased, and she resides in Reading; Jonathan m. Clara Moll, and 
2 CONC died in Reading leaving no children; and six children died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elias Zerbe, son of John, born Aug. 4, 1822, was a resident of North Heidelberg for a number of years, and in 1870 came to Reading where he followed the carpenter's trade until within a few years of his death, March 25, 1906. Both
2 CONC  he and his wife are buried in the Charles Evans cemetery. On Nov. 7, 1846, he married Mary Ann Moyer, daughter of George Moyer and his wife Catherine Gerber (1799-1860). To this union were born children as follows: Emma, born Sep
2 CONC t. 16, 1847, died at the age of eleven years; Anna, born March 3, 1852, died March 5, 1852; Levi M., born Dec. 3, 1853; James M. born Sept. 27, 1855, lives in Reading; Harrison, born Jan. 9, 1859; George McClellan, born Jan. 25, 1
2 CONC 864.
2 CONT
2 CONT Levi M. Zerbe, son of Elias, was born in Marion township Dec. 3, 1853. He learned the carpenter's trade when he was eighteen and this he has followed ever since. For two years he was engaged as a millwright in Reading, to which ci
2 CONC ty he came in the fall of 1871. In 1875 he began working for the Reading Railway Company, and continued there until 1879, when he was appointed on the police force by Mayor Henry Tyson, but at the change of administration two year
2 CONC s later he resigned and went back to the car shops, following his trade there until 1885. The next two years found his working as a millwright with Elias Schmehl, of Reading, but in the latter part of 1887 he again returned to the
2 CONC  Railway Company, and has since continued there. On March 16, 1897, he was made foreman of the planing mill department, having some eighty-seven men in his employ. He is a member of the Relief Association, and also belongs to the 
2 CONC American Mechanics, No. 27, of Reading; the Royal Arcanum, No. 495; and the Schuylkill Fire Company. He is a member of St. Luke's Lutheran Church, and is connected with the Trinity Lutheran Brotherhood of Reading. In political aff
2 CONC airs he is a Democrat. On May 15, 1875, Mr. Zerbe was married to Amelia A. Werner, daughter of Frederick Werner, of Reading. Three children have been born to them, namely: Lillie M. , who died in infancy; Anna M. , at home; and Em
2 CONC ma N. (1878-1900), deceased wife of Isaac Mengel, of Reading.
2 CONT
2 CONT Urias Zerby, son of John and brother of Elias, was born Feb. 16, 1834, and died Jan. 6, 1907, and is buried in the Charles Evans cemetery. For several years he followed farming in Exeter township, and then moved to Muhlenberg town
2 CONC ship, where he lived until his removal to Reading April 1, 1875. He farmed the John Epler farm now all built up and improved as a part of the city along Schuylkill avenue. He married Lovina Snyder, who bore him the following child
2 CONC ren: William A., John, James, Missouri, Irwin, Frank, Mamie and Amanda.
2 CONT
2 CONT William A. Zerby, son of Urias, and now a well known citizen of Reading engaged in the milk business, was born in Muhlenberg township May 25, 1865. He attended the township schools, and was ten years old when the father removed of
2 CONC  Reading. Here in the city he attended the public schools, in the meantime assisting his father on the farm. He was a letter carrier for three years, at the end of that time engaging in the dairy business at No. 639 Schuylkill ave
2 CONC nue, where he has built up a large trade.. In his political principle Mr. Zerby is a Democrat, and has served as a member of the county committee. He was elected collector for the Fifteenth ward in 1908. In his religious faith, li
2 CONC ke all his family, he is a Lutheran and belongs to Hope Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Oct. 8, 1887, Mr. Zerby was united in marriage with Miss Missouri De Long, daughter of Jacob De Long, of Lehigh county. Their children are: Arthur, Martha, William, Harry, Lester, Hilda and Esther. Mr. Zerby is highly respected
2 CONC  wherever he is known. 
