0 @N6000000002188440991@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT      The election in 1619 of Elector Friedrich V as King of Bohemia precipitated the Thirty Years War, in which the Palatinate (Pfalz) was ravaged both by the imperial forces under Tilly and by the Protestant army under Mansfeld. 
1 CONC The Upper Palatinate and the electoral vote were taken from Friedrich and transferred to Bavaria, but at the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 a new vote was created for Friedrich's successor, Karl I Ludwig, and the Rhenish lands, devas
1 CONC tated in the war, were returned to his control. The Upper Palatinate remained a part of Bavaria.   The Protestant diet of Bohemia deposed the Roman Catholic King Ferdinand (Holy Roman Emperor Ferdinand II) and chose Prince Friedri
1 CONC ch V of Pfalz as King of Bohemia.  Influenced by his minister Prince Christian of Anhalt, Friedrich accepted but did not receive the aid expected from his father-in-law, King James I of England, and from the Protestant Union again
1 CONC st Ferdinand. After initial success, his supporters were routed at White Mountain in 1620. Friedrich thus lost Bohemia; from his short tenure came the derisive name, the Winter King. He was put under imperial ban and was stripped 
1 CONC of all his remaining territories. The electorate was transferred to Maximilian I of Bavaria. These struggles were the first campaigns of the Thirty Years War. The Hanoverian kings of England were descended from Friedrich and his w
1 CONC ife, Elizabeth, through their daughter Sophia, who was the mother of King George I of England.
